.TH TUXTOYS 1 2026-10-12 "TuxToys" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tuxtoys \- power-user utilities for Linux Mint Cinnamon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tuxtoys awake on
.RB [ \-\-for
.I DURATION
|
.B \-\-until
.IR HH:MM ]
.RB [ \-\-screen " | " \-\-no\-screen ]
.br
.B tuxtoys awake
.RB { off | toggle | status }
.br
.B tuxtoys lightswitch
.RB [ status " | " toggle ]
.br
.B tuxtoys alwaysontop opacity
.RB { up | down }
.br
.B tuxtoys hosts
.br
.B tuxtoys env
.br
.B tuxtoys colorpicker
.br
.B tuxtoys screenruler
.br
.B tuxtoys textextractor
.br
.B tuxtoys shortcutguide
.br
.B tuxtoys findmymouse
.br
.B tuxtoys modules
.RB [ list " | " enable
.I MODULE
|
.B disable
.IR MODULE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tuxtoys
controls TuxToys from a terminal or a script. It talks to the TuxToys service of the
current session, which starts on its own if it is not running yet.
.SS Awake
Awake keeps the computer from going to sleep, without changing the power settings. Every
.B awake
command prints the state it leaves Awake in.
.TP
.B on
Keeps the computer awake until it is turned off, or for a time, or until a time of day.
.TP
.B off
Lets the computer sleep again as the power settings say.
.TP
.B toggle
Turns Awake off when it is on, and on as set in TuxToys when it is off. This is the
command to put on a keyboard shortcut.
.TP
.B status
Prints whether Awake is on, and for how long.
.SS Light Switch
Light Switch switches the desktop between Linux Mint's dark mode and its day mode (Mixed
or Light), the way the Themes window does, by Night Light's schedule or at set times. Both
commands print the mode the desktop is in, today's dark hours and its keyboard shortcut.
.PP
When it comes on, Light Switch adds a custom keyboard shortcut to Cinnamon,
.BR Super+Ctrl+Shift+D ,
named
.BR "TuxToys: Light Switch" ,
unless those keys are in use already. Its keys can be changed in the Keyboard settings.
It is taken away when Light Switch, or its shortcut, is switched off in TuxToys.
.TP
.B status
Prints the mode and the dark hours, and why Light Switch cannot switch the desktop if it
cannot. The default.
.TP
.B toggle
Switches to dark now, or back to the day's mode, until the schedule next changes. This is
the command to put on a keyboard shortcut.
.SS Always on Top
Always on Top keeps a window above the others, with Muffin's own
.B Super+Ctrl+T
shortcut, and draws a frame around it.
.PP
When it comes on, it adds two custom keyboard shortcuts to Cinnamon,
.B Super+Ctrl+Page Up
and
.BR "Super+Ctrl+Page Down" ,
which run the command below, unless those keys are in use already.
.TP
.BR "opacity up" ", " "opacity down"
Makes the active window one step more or less opaque, if it is kept above the others.
Needs python3-xlib, and works without the TuxToys service.
.SS Hosts File Editor
.TP
.B hosts
Opens Hosts File Editor, which edits
.IR /etc/hosts .
Saving asks for an administrator's password through
.BR pkexec (1).
Hosts File Editor has to be switched on.
.SS Environment Variables
.TP
.B env
Opens Environment Variables, which sets the user's variables, profiles of them, and
the system's in
.IR /etc/environment .
Environment Variables has to be switched on.
.SS Color Picker
.TP
.B colorpicker
Picks a color from anywhere on the screen and copies it, or opens Color Picker's
editor first, as set. It is the command Color Picker's keyboard shortcut,
.BR Super+Shift+C ,
runs. Color Picker has to be switched on.
.SS Screen Ruler
.TP
.B screenruler
Opens Screen Ruler over the screen, to measure what is on it. It is the command
Screen Ruler's keyboard shortcut,
.BR Super+Ctrl+Shift+M ,
runs. Screen Ruler has to be switched on.
.SS Text Extractor
.TP
.B textextractor
Opens Text Extractor over the screen: the text in the region dragged over, or the word
clicked, is read by
.BR tesseract (1)
and copied. It is the command Text Extractor's keyboard shortcut,
.BR Super+Shift+T ,
runs. Text Extractor has to be switched on, and tesseract installed with at least one
language.
.SS Shortcut Guide
.TP
.B shortcutguide
Opens Shortcut Guide, the list of Cinnamon's keyboard shortcuts, TuxToys' and the active
app's; run again while it is open, it puts it away. It is the command Shortcut Guide's
keyboard shortcut,
.BR Super+Shift+/ ,
runs. Shortcut Guide has to be switched on.
.SS Find My Mouse
.TP
.B findmymouse
Shines Find My Mouse's spotlight on the pointer, or puts it away when it is showing. It
is the command Find My Mouse's keyboard shortcut,
.BR Super+Shift+F ,
runs when it is set to start by a shortcut rather than by the Ctrl key or a shake.
Find My Mouse has to be switched on.
.SS Modules
.TP
.B list
Lists the modules, and whether each is on, off, or failed and why. The default.
.TP
.BI enable " MODULE"
Switches a module on, and keeps it on at the next login.
.TP
.BI disable " MODULE"
Switches a module off, and keeps it off.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-for " DURATION"
Stays awake this long: minutes (\fB90\fR or \fB90m\fR), hours (\fB2h\fR), or both
(\fB1h30m\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-until " HH:MM"
Stays awake until this time of day; a time already past today means tomorrow.
.TP
.BR \-\-screen ", " \-\-no\-screen
Keeps the screen on as well, or lets it turn off while the computer stays awake. Without
either, the setting from the panel icon applies.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 1
TuxToys could not do it: it is not running, the module is switched off, or it refused
the request. The reason is printed.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is wrong.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxtoys/config.json
The settings, one section per module.
.TP
.I /var/backups/tuxtoys/
Hosts File Editor's backups of
.IR /etc/hosts ,
removed with the package when it is purged, and Environment Variables' of
.IR /etc/environment .
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxtoys/environment
The user's variables and the applied profile's, written by Environment Variables and
loaded by a line it adds to
.I ~/.xsessionrc
and
.IR ~/.bashrc .
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxtoys/shortcutguide/*.json
The user's own lists for Shortcut Guide, one file per app, read each time it opens. Its
settings page adds and edits them; they can be written by hand too:
.RS
.EX
{
  "version": 1,
  "name": "GIMP",
  "icon": "gimp",
  "windows": ["gimp"],
  "groups": [
    {"title": "Layers",
     "items": [{"id": "new-layer", "label": "New layer",
                "keys": ["<Primary><Shift>n"]}]}
  ]
}
.EE
.RE
.IP
.I windows
are the class part of the app's WM_CLASS, as
.B xprop WM_CLASS
shows it, and
.I keys
are written as Cinnamon's settings write them. A list for an app Shortcut Guide already
knows adds its groups after the app's own. A file that cannot be read is left out, and
the reason is logged.
.SH NOTES
Removing the package cannot reach into a user's own settings: a keyboard shortcut a tool
added stays in Cinnamon's custom shortcuts until the tool is switched off first, or the
shortcut is removed in the Keyboard settings. Likewise, Nemo's Rename on several items
keeps opening Rename+, if Rename+ was set to take it over, until Rename+ is switched off,
and the lines Environment Variables adds to
.I ~/.xsessionrc
and
.I ~/.bashrc
stay until it is switched off.
.SH EXAMPLES
Stay awake for an hour and a half, with the screen on:
.PP
.RS
tuxtoys awake on \-\-for 1h30m \-\-screen
.RE
.PP
Stay awake until six in the evening:
.PP
.RS
tuxtoys awake on \-\-until 18:00
.RE
.PP
Switch Light Switch on, then go dark now:
.PP
.RS
tuxtoys modules enable lightswitch
.br
tuxtoys lightswitch toggle
.RE
